{'type': 'text', 'text': '{
  "stdout": "422 /app/research/report.md\n---\n# Project THOR (Terrestrial Hazard Operational Response) - Sub-task 7: UAS-Based Granular Material Dispersion for Environmental Remediation\n\n## Title\n\n**AMPS-D: Acousto-Mechanical Phase-Transition Dispersion System for Autonomous UAS Payload Delivery**\n\n*A Novel Ultrasonically-Assisted Helical Centrifugal Dispersal Mechanism for Granular Material Application*\n\n---\n\n## Background & Motivation\n\n### 1.1 Problem Statement\n\nThe ongoing environmental remediation challenges at former nuclear research sites and active contamination zones require rapid, uniform deployment of remediation materials over large areas. The Fukushima Daiichi cleanup efforts have highlighted the critical need for aerial delivery systems capable of dispersing neutron-absorbing and contaminant-binding materials with high spatial precision. Current aerial dispersal methods—including drop-chutes, rotating drum spreaders, and basic vibratory feeders—suffer from three fundamental limitations:\n\n1. **Non-uniform dispersion patterns** resulting in overlapping over-application zones and uncovered gaps\n2. **Material bridging and rat-holing** in hopper systems, especially for fine, low-cohesion powders like Zeolite\n3. **Inability to dynamically adjust** spread geometry mid-flight to compensate for wind shear and UAV position variations\n\n### 1.2 Current State of the Art\n\nTraditional agricultural crop-dusting aircraft utilize spinning disc spreaders with fixed blade geometry, producing elliptical dispersion patterns that are difficult to scale for precision environmental remediation. Existing UAS dispersal mechanisms predominantly employ:\n\n- **Gravity-fed drop systems** with simple gates or sluices, providing poor spatial resolution\n- **Rotating drum feeders** that generate inconsistent flow rates due to material compaction\n- **Belt conveyors** that are mechanically complex, heavy, and prone to jamming with fine powders\n- **Standard vibratory feeders** that lack active agglomerate-breaking capability for moisture-sensitive materials\n\nNone of these mechanisms incorporate active flow-conditioning to address the rheological challenges of fine, low-cohesion powders in microgravity flight environments.\n",
  "stderr": "",
